'''Italy v Brazil''' was a football match that took place between [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] and [[Italy national football team|Italy]] at [[Estadi de Sarrià|Estadio Sarriá]], [[Barcelona]] on 5 July 1982. It was the final second round [[group stage]] match for Group C in the [[1982 FIFA World Cup]]. The match was won by Italy 3–2, with Italian [[Forward (association football)|striker]] [[Paolo Rossi]] scoring a [[hat-trick]]. The result eliminated Brazil from the tournament while Italy would go on to win it. The match has been described as one of the greatest in the history of association football.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Duarte|first1=Fernando|title=Brazil lost that Italy game in 1982 but won a place in history – Falcão|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/may/30/brazil-falcao-1982-world-cup-italy|website=The Guardian|access-date=20 May 2015|date=30 May 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last1=Wilson|first1=Jonathan|title=Italy 3-2 Brazil, 1982: the day naivety, not football itself, died|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2012/jul/25/italy-brazil-1982|website=The Guardian|access-date=20 May 2015|date=25 July 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last1=Lewis|first1=Tim|title=1982: Why Brazil V Italy Was One Of Football's Greatest Ever Matches|url=http://www.esquire.com/uk/culture/news/a6396/1982-why-brazil-v-italy-was-one-of-footballs-greatest-ever-matches/|website=Esquire|access-date=10 February 2018|date=11 July 2014|archive-date=12 June 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210612215052/https://www.esquire.com/uk/culture/news/a6396/1982-why-brazil-v-italy-was-one-of-footballs-greatest-ever-matches/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

==Pre-match==
[[file:Argentina v brazil 1982 01.jpg|thumb|left|Prior to this match, Brazil had won all of their games. In the image, the victory over Argentina]]
Brazil had won all three of their [[1982 FIFA World Cup Group 6|first group stage]] matches, including comprehensive wins over [[Scotland national football team|Scotland]] (4–1) and [[New Zealand men's national football team|New Zealand]] (4–0), and were the pre-tournament favourites.<ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.cbc.ca/sports/worldcup2006/history/events/1982.html |title=1982 Spain |publisher=Canadian Broadcasting Corporation |access-date=22 June 2013 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081216135130/http://www.cbc.ca/sports/worldcup2006/history/events/1982.html |archive-date=16 December 2008 | work=CBC News}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Rossi wakes to flatten favourites|url=https://www.fifa.com/classicfootball/matches/match=788/index.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080303121751/http://www.fifa.com/classicfootball/matches/match=788/index.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=3 March 2008|access-date=30 June 2011|newspaper=FIFA}}</ref> They were widely praised for their attacking style. In their initial [[1982 FIFA World Cup Group C|second group stag]]e match, Brazil beat South American rivals and World Cup holders [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]] 3–1.

Italy, meanwhile, had a slow start to the tournament, having drawn all three of their [[1982 FIFA World Cup Group 1|first group stage]] matches and finishing runners-up in their group; they had only qualified for the second group stage by having scored one more goal than third-placed [[Cameroon national football team|Cameroon]], who also finished with three draws. However, in the first of their second-round matches, Italy recorded an impressive 2–1 victory over Argentina.

Rossi had failed to score up to that point and there was considerable debate about whether he should be in the team, as he had only recently returned from a two-year ban following his involvement in the [[Totonero 1980]] match-fixing scandal (his banning was initially ruled to last for three years, but was reduced on appeal). Italy were forced to play for a win to reach the semi-final, due to their inferior goal difference.

==Match==

===Summary===
{{multiple image
