==Impact==
[[File:Paolo Rossi at the 1982 FIFA World Cup.jpg|thumb|200px|Italian striker Paolo Rossi was the [[Player of the match|man-of-the-match]], having scored three goals]]
The result was seen by many as not only a defeat for Brazil, but a defeat of their attacking philosophy by the more organised Italians.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/columns/story/_/id/897472/rewind-to-1982%3A-brilliant-brazil%27s-brush-with-greatness?cc=5739 |title=Rewind to 1982: Brilliant Brazil's brush with greatness – ESPN Soccernet |publisher=[[ESPN]] |date=24 March 2011 |access-date=9 July 2012 |archive-date=7 December 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111207015210/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/columns/story/_/id/897472/rewind-to-1982%3A-brilliant-brazil%27s-brush-with-greatness?cc=5739 |url-status=dead }}</ref>
This match has since then been labelled by the Brazilian press as the "Sarrià Tragedy" (Portuguese: ''Tragédia do Sarrià'').<ref>{{cite web |url=http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/2007/writers/tim_vickery/07/24/brazil.reax/1.html |title=The great debate (cont'd) |publisher=Sportsillustrated.cnn.com |date=24 July 2007 |access-date=6 December 2012 |archive-date=18 March 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090318025907/http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/2007/writers/tim_vickery/07/24/brazil.reax/1.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> The result of the match had a profound and lasting impact on Brazilian football and fundamentally changed its philosophy.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www1.folha.uol.com.br/fsp/esporte/fk1705200208.htm |title=Com derrota, nasceu futebol de resultados |date=17 May 2002 |access-date=11 April 2020}}</ref> According to Luizinho, Brazil's centre back in 1982, the defeat changed Brazilian coaches' way of thinking, leading to a new, destructive philosophy based on more tactical, physical, defensive, and counterattacking football, possessing similarities with the style of football played by the Italians against the Brazilians.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://ndmais.com.br/esportes/35-anos-de-sarria-o-impacto-da-copa-de-1982-no-futebol-mundial/ |title=35 anos de Sarriá: O impacto da Copa de 1982 no futebol mundial |date=24 March 2011 |access-date=11 April 2020 |archive-date=9 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210509091501/https://ndmais.com.br/esportes/35-anos-de-sarria-o-impacto-da-copa-de-1982-no-futebol-mundial/amp/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>

As described by sports journalist Tim Vickery: "For many Brazilian coaches, the failure of that 1982 side to win the World Cup (...) served as proof for ideas that had been kicking around for a while – starting with a 5–1 massacre at the hands of Belgium in 1963, confirmed by the defeat by Holland in the 1974 World Cup. The physical development of the game, it was thought, meant that traditional methods had to be revised. Brazilian players had to bulk up – Rubens Minelli, the most successful domestic coach of the 70s, wanted his team to be made up of six footers. And with less space on the field, the future of football lay in the counter attack, rather than elaborate attempts to pass through midfield." Tim Vickery continues by stating that "these thoughts have carried a lot of weight in the Brazilian game. They help explain why a succession of Brazil sides have caught the eye for explosive breaks down the flanks rather than for the succession of midfield triangles that enraptured Cappa and everyone else in 1982. When former Middlesbrough left-back Branco was in charge of Brazil’s youth sides, he told me that right from the start of the process the search for big, strong youngsters was a priority. Brazilian coaches, meanwhile, became fond of spouting the statistic that the chances of a goal are reduced if the move contains more than seven passes."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/blogs/timvickery/2011/08/brazil_have_never_looked_the_s.html |title=Brazil fail to rediscover winning formula |date=15 August 2011 |access-date=11 April 2020}}</ref> He also earlier stated in 2006 that "had Brazil won the trophy in 1982 the team would be more than a fond memory. They might be the blueprint for future sides, because winners are always copied."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/4937698.stm |title=Football is the poorer for the passing last week of Tele Santana. |date=24 April 2006 |access-date=11 April 2020}}</ref> When describing the events of the match, Sócrates later recounted: "We had a hell of a team and played with happiness. Then Rossi had three touches and scored a hat-trick. Football as we know it died on that day."

Further success in 1994 and 2002 in mostly pragmatic, less flashy styles cemented the new philosophy and practically buried the traditional passing style further into the past.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.si.com/.amp/more-sports/2009/08/04/brazil-usa |title=As Cup looms, Brazil hopes to extend magic ride that began in '94. |date=4 August 2009 |access-date=12 April 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uol.com.br/esporte/futebol/ultimas-noticias/2009/07/17/ult59u199801.jhtm |title=Brasil de 94 consagra pragmatismo e espalha tendência de 'marcação total'. |date=17 July 2009 |access-date=12 April 2020}}</ref>

The rise of [[tiki-taka]], a style of football partly based on moving triangles, positional interchange and intricate passing – highly reminiscent of the old Brazilian passing style, embodied by the 1982 team – has helped recover some of the prestige of the 1982 team in the country. The crushing defeat of the Brazilian counterattacking style by tiki-taka teams, namely [[2011 FIFA Club World Cup Final|the 4–0 defeat of Santos to Barcelona]] and the [[Brazil v Germany (2014 FIFA World Cup)|7–1 Brazilian defeat to Germany at home at the World Cup in 2014]] laid bare how Brazilian football had been left behind.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://grahamhunter.tv/night-team-died/ |title=ZERO SYMPATHY FOR SCOLARI AND BRAZIL'S THUGGISH TACTICS. |date=9 July 2014 |access-date=12 April 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/blogs/timvickery/2011/12/what_brazil_can_learn_from_bar.html |title=What Brazil can learn from Barcelona. |date=18 December 2011 |access-date=12 April 2020}}</ref> As a result, the country's football philosophy has been slowly returning to the old intricate passing style in the last few years, as recent success by [[2017 Copa Libertadores Finals|Grêmio]] and [[2019 Clube de Regatas do Flamengo season|Flamengo]] have shown.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www1.folha.uol.com.br/colunas/tostao/2020/01/dez-anos-de-soberba-e-orgulho.shtml |title=Dez anos de soberba do Brasil no futebol. |date=1 January 2020 |access-date=12 April 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.worldsoccer.com/features/jorge-jesus-revolution-flamengo-continues-tim-vickery-408786/amp |title=Jorge Jesus Revolution at Flamengo Continues |author=Tim Vickery. |date=7 October 2019 |access-date=12 April 2020 |archive-date=12 April 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200412164430/https://www.worldsoccer.com/features/jorge-jesus-revolution-flamengo-continues-tim-vickery-408786/amp |url-status=dead }}</ref>

